n I first started  out quite a logical why would they write  it that way and now having struggled  with more difficult texts I go back to  my beginning lessons in a blink here and  and it really I feel that I'm learning  every word every word that I'd forgotten  because it's now so easy for me because  I pushed myself harder to go forward and  and I guess what I'm driving at is you  start somewhere in the language with  some lessons you're gonna have to go  back there again you should push  yourself to do some more difficult  lessons it's kind of anarchic and it's  not unlike the relationships it's like  like having a romance you know you don't  begin day one asking your partner where  was she born what does her father do you  know you got the basics like less than  one we do this listen to it's a matter  of you know it just comes at you however  it comes at you you have to deal with it  and then it's pleasant to go back and of  course there's an expression in French  oh well yeah to zoo I say pull me as I  move which means you already go back to  your first love but in the case of  language learning yeah you should go  back to some of the lessons you started  with and it's surprising how much more  you pick up on your second pass thinking  again of French poetry those the famous  ballad they dumb detours IDs where  there's the expression whose song don't  I think lineage don't I also don't pass  a lineage donto like where are the snows  of yesteryear  and of course the snows of yesteryear  are no more but when you're studying  language that's not the case because  your first lessons are still there you  can go back to them and you should go  back to them and I think I've mentioned  this before it's important very the  difficulty of what you're doing  don't try to ace the easy lessons at the  beginning because you won't be able to  keep forging forward creating a new  path in the snow so to speak but then  when you go back to that well trodden  path you'll find it e